### 1. Introduction

This report summarises the core functions of the network layer as presented in Lectures 13–16. It draws directly on the lecture slides and notes to describe the principles of best‑effort delivery, the separation of the data and control planes, CIDR addressing and subnetting, longest‑prefix match forwarding, and link‑state routing with Dijkstra’s algorithm. These concepts form the foundation of the Internet’s ability to route packets across heterogeneous networks at scale.

